<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-chain-rule">
    <title>The Chain Rule</title>
    <p>
        The Chain Rule is a fundamental differentiation rule that allows us to find the derivative of composite functions. If we have a function defined as <m>h(x) = f(g(x))</m>, where <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are differentiable functions, then the derivative of <m>h</m> with respect to <m>x</m> is given by:
        <me>h'(x) = f'(g(x)) \cdot g'(x)</me>
    </p>
    <p>
        In other words, to differentiate a composite function, we first differentiate the outer function <m>f</m> with respect to its argument <m>g(x)</m>, and then multiply by the derivative of the inner function <m>g</m> with respect to <m>x</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
        The Chain Rule is essential for differentiating a wide variety of functions, including those involving exponentials, logarithms, trigonometric functions, and more complex compositions. It allows us to break down complicated functions into simpler parts, making differentiation more manageable.
    </p>
    <p>
        For example, if we want to differentiate <m>h(x) = \sin(x^2)</m>, we can identify <m>f(u) = \sin(u)</m> and <m>g(x) = x^2</m>. Applying the Chain Rule, we get:
        <md>
          <mrow>h'(x) \amp = \cos(x^2) \cdot 2x</mrow>
        </md>
    </p>
    <p>
        The Chain Rule is a powerful tool that extends our ability to differentiate beyond simple functions, enabling us to handle a wide range of mathematical models and applications in physics, engineering, economics, and other fields.
    </p>
    <example>
    <statement>
      <p><m>f(x)=e^{2x}</m></p>  
    </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      To find the derivative of <m>f(x) = e^{2x}</m>, we can use the Chain Rule. Let <m>u = 2x</m>, so that <m>f(x) = e^u</m>. 
      Then, we have:
        <md>
            <mrow>f'(x) \amp = \frac{d}{dx}(e^u) \cdot \frac{du}{dx}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = e^u \cdot 2</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = 2e^{2x}</mrow>
        </md>
    </p>    
    </solution>
</example>




</section>